---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: traefik
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
  annotations:
    # The annotation, not spec.loadBalancerIP — that field is deprecated upstream and MetalLB
    # reads this. This is the only way to claim an address out of the autoAssign: false
    # ingress-pool (roles/setup/k3s/templates/metallb-pool.yaml.j2).
    #
    # metallb.universe.tf/, NOT metallb.io/. metallb.io is the API group of the CRDs
    # (IPAddressPool, L2Advertisement); Service annotations keep the older universe.tf
    # namespace. Kubernetes accepts any annotation key, and MetalLB ignores ones it does not
    # recognise, so the wrong prefix is silent: the Service is created, an address is
    # assigned from the auto-assign pool instead of the reserved VIP, and nothing anywhere
    # reports a problem. MetalLB's own metallb.universe.tf/ip-allocated-from-pool annotation
    # on the resulting Service is what gives it away.
    metallb.universe.tf/loadBalancerIPs: {{ k3s_metallb_ingress_vip }}
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  # Local, NOT the Cluster default, and this is a security control rather than a tuning knob.
  # Under Cluster the node masquerades inbound traffic, so Traefik sees every client as
  # cni0's 10.42.0.1 — which is inside 10.0.0.0/8, so Authelia's access_control rules would
  # score ALL traffic, including public traffic after slice 6, as RFC1918 LAN and hand it the
  # one_factor policy meant for the local network. Local preserves the real client address.
  # Safe here because Traefik is the only ingress and the cluster is single-node; revisit the
  # scheduling implications when daniel-server joins at slice 7.
  externalTrafficPolicy: Local
  # The node's OWN address as a second front door (slice-6 B5, 2026-08-09). The router's
  # port-forward UI only targets devices from its DHCP/ARP list, and the MetalLB VIP has no
  # lease and never will — so the 80/443 forward points at the DEVICE daniel-box, and
  # kube-proxy answers on the node IP for this Service. The VIP stays the LAN-facing name
  # (Pi-hole overrides, wg-easy, bridge transports all keep using .240); this field exists
  # for the WAN path only. Local policy applies to externalIPs the same way, so client
  # addresses stay real. Revisit at slice-7 join: the forward follows whichever node this
  # IP belongs to.
  externalIPs:
    - {{ hostvars['daniel-box'].server_ip }}
  selector:
    app: traefik
  ports:
    - name: http
      port: 80
      targetPort: http
    - name: https
      port: 443
      targetPort: https
